## Proof

We use the **Schwarz–Pick lemma**. For any analytic function $f : \mathbb{D} \to \mathbb{D}$, the Schwarz–Pick lemma gives, at every point $z \in \mathbb{D}$,

$$\frac{|f'(z)|}{1 - |f(z)|^2} \leq \frac{1}{1 - |z|^2}.$$

Evaluating at $z = 0$ yields the classical Schwarz lemma estimate:

$$|f'(0)| \leq 1 - |f(0)|^2. \tag{1}$$

Applying (1) to each $f_n$:

$$|f_n'(0)| \leq 1 - |f_n(0)|^2,$$

which we rearrange as

$$|f_n(0)|^2 \leq 1 - |f_n'(0)|. \tag{2}$$

By hypothesis, $|f_n'(0)| \to 1$ as $n \to \infty$. Taking the limit in (2):

$$\limsup_{n \to \infty} |f_n(0)|^2 \leq 1 - \lim_{n \to \infty} |f_n'(0)| = 1 - 1 = 0.$$

Since $|f_n(0)|^2 \geq 0$, we conclude

$$\lim_{n \to \infty} |f_n(0)|^2 = 0, \qquad \text{hence} \qquad \lim_{n \to \infty} |f_n(0)| = 0.$$

A complex sequence converging to $0$ in absolute value converges to $0$ itself. Therefore

$$\boxed{\lim_{n \to \infty} f_n(0) = 0.}$$

**Remark on sharpness.** The bound is attained in the limit by the rotations $f_n(z) = e^{i\theta_n} z$, for which $f_n'(0) = e^{i\theta_n}$ (so $|f_n'(0)| = 1$) and $f_n(0) = 0$. More generally, functions approaching the extremal case of equality in Schwarz–Pick must have their value at the origin driven to $0$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
